<h2>The prosecution case</h2>
<p>The FIR — No. 118/2025, Police Station Hauz Qazi — alleged a robbery at gunpoint in which approximately Rs 60 lakh was taken. The applicant, Ravi Gupta @ Ganja, was arraigned with co-accused persons under provisions of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 — including Section 309(4) (robbery), Section 311, Section 317(2) (receiving stolen property), Section 61(2) (criminal conspiracy), Section 238 and Section 3(5) — together with Sections 25 and 27 of the Arms Act, 1959. The State opposed bail on two planks: CCTV footage said to capture the incident, and recoveries of cash and jewellery stated to have been effected at the applicant&rsquo;s instance during the investigation.</p>

<h2>What tilted the balance</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Main role elsewhere.</strong> The principal role in the robbery was attributed by the prosecution itself to a co-accused — and that co-accused was already on bail.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>No identification.</strong> No eyewitness identified the applicant as a participant in the incident, leaving the CCTV footage to do work it could only complete at trial.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Parity precedent within the case.</strong> Another co-accused, too, had been granted bail, so the applicant&rsquo;s continued custody stood out against the treatment of the rest of the array.</div>
</div>
<p>Granting bail on a personal bond of Rs 25,000 with one surety, the Court applied the familiar logic of parity: when the allegedly graver participant is at liberty, keeping a co-accused with a lesser attributed role in custody requires something specific — stronger evidence against him, worse antecedents, or a distinct risk of flight or tampering. The State&rsquo;s material did not supply that something. The recoveries, whose legal character and evidentiary value would be tested through the trial, did not by themselves displace the parity claim.</p>

<h2>Parity: how courts actually apply it</h2>
<p>Parity is often invoked and often misunderstood. It is not a rule that bail to one accused entitles every co-accused to release. Courts compare the role attributed to each accused in the FIR and chargesheet, the recoveries and identifications specific to each, and their individual antecedents. Parity succeeds when that comparison shows the bail applicant standing on the same or better footing than a co-accused already enlarged. It fails when the applicant&rsquo;s role is distinct — the armed participant as against the lookout, the conspirator who planned as against the one who merely received property.</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Factor compared</th><th>What courts look for</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Attributed role</td><td>Specific overt acts in the FIR and chargesheet; who is said to have wielded the weapon, who kept watch, who received property</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Evidence specific to the accused</td><td>Identification by eyewitnesses or test identification parade; recoveries and their link to the offence</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Antecedents</td><td>Prior involvements, previous convictions, conduct on earlier bails</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Risk factors</td><td>Flight risk, capacity to influence witnesses, stage of trial</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Takeaways for practice</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Map the array before arguing.</strong> A parity argument begins with a table of co-accused, their attributed roles, and the orders granting or refusing them bail — presented to the court, not left to be assembled from the record.</p>
  <p><strong>Identification gaps matter.</strong> Where no eyewitness names the applicant and no test identification parade was held, CCTV footage alone is a question for trial, not a conclusive answer at the bail stage.</p>
  <p><strong>Recoveries are not a veto.</strong> Recoveries at the accused&rsquo;s instance are relevant, but their weight is assessed alongside the rest of the material; they do not automatically defeat parity where the comparative position otherwise favours release.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises a judgment of the Delhi High Court for general information. It is not legal advice, and the bail outcome in any matter turns on its own facts and record.</div>
